        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Sealing cracks in a driveway is a cheap, high-return job, but only if you treat it as joint sealing rather than gap filling. The goal is not to pack the crack full of material. It is to bond a flexible bead to two clean walls, at the right depth, so it can stretch every winter and squeeze every summer without tearing loose. Get the product, the prep and the depth right, and a ten-dollar tube buys you years. Get them wrong, and you will be digging out the same crack next spring.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="know-your-pavement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Start With the Pavement: Concrete and Asphalt Need Different Products
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first decision has nothing to do with brands. Concrete and asphalt move differently, bond differently and fail differently, and the products made for each are not interchangeable. Concrete is rigid and cracks because it shrinks as it cures and because the ground under it moves. Asphalt is flexible, softens in summer heat, and cracks mostly because the binder oxidizes and goes brittle with age and sun. A sealant that suits one usually fails on the other within a season or two.
          </p>

          <p>
            On concrete, the workhorse is a polyurethane or hybrid polymer sealant: a one-part product that cures into a tough rubber, bonds hard to clean concrete, and stretches with the slab. On asphalt, the standard materials are rubberized asphalt crack fillers, either cold-pour emulsions sold in jugs and tubes or hot-pour rubberized rope and block fillers that are melted and poured. Asphalt fillers are designed to blend with the binder in the pavement. Polyurethane on asphalt can work in a pinch, but it does not bond as well to oily, flexing asphalt and it looks out of place once the driveway is sealcoated.
          </p>

          <p>
            Going the other way is worse. Rubberized asphalt filler poured into a concrete driveway crack stays soft and tacky in the heat, tracks onto shoes and tires, stains the surrounding concrete, and leaves a black line that no concrete sealer will cover. If your driveway is concrete, keep asphalt products off it entirely. If you are not sure what you have, look at an edge or a chip: concrete shows gray cement paste and stone, asphalt shows black binder coating every piece of stone.
          </p>

          <p>
            This guide covers both, with the concrete method first because it is the one people most often get wrong. If you have an asphalt driveway and want product picks, our <a href="/best-asphalt-crack-filler/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">asphalt crack filler guide</a> and <a href="/best-driveway-crack-filler/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">driveway crack filler roundup</a> cover the specific products. For the wider picture on concrete, including epoxy, patching mortar and structural cracks, see our main <a href="/concrete-crack-repair/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete crack repair guide</a>.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Driveway</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best material</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Avoid</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Polyurethane or hybrid polymer sealant, self-leveling or gun-grade</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Asphalt emulsion, rigid patch mortar in moving cracks</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Asphalt</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rubberized cold-pour or hot-pour crack filler</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rigid concrete patch, silicone</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Either, crack wider than 1 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Patch or repair, not sealant alone</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Filling the full depth with sealant</td>
