        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Sealing cracks in a driveway is one of the few home repairs where the method matters more than the product. Pick a sealant made for your pavement, clean the crack until the walls are bare, set the depth with backer rod or sand, and seal on a mild, dry day. That routine will outlast any premium tube applied to a dirty, overfilled crack.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Know where sealing stops. Cracks wider than an inch, edges that crumble, and slabs that have dropped or lifted need repair or leveling first. For everything narrower, a careful afternoon and a couple of tubes of polyurethane will keep water out of the base and add years to the driveway.
          </p>
